In any triangle the orthocentre is the intersection of the three altitudes.  \nFrom (3) one altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AB\\).  \nTherefore the orthocentre \\(H\\) belongs to that line:\n\n\\[\nH\\in\\ell_{H},\\qquad \\ell_{H}\\;:\\;\\text{line through }P\\text{ parallel to }AB .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Reformulation of the required tangency  \n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nThe tangent\u2013chord theorem states that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) iff  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), (5) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BFE .}\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus the original problem is reduced to proving the directed\u2011angle equality (6).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  An inversion centred at \\(A\\)\n\nPerform an inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary radius.  \nRecall the standard facts:\n\n* a circle passing through the centre of inversion is sent to a line not passing through the centre;\n* a point \\(X\\neq A\\) is sent to the point \\(X'\\) lying on the ray \\(AX\\) such that \\(AX\\cdot AX'=k^{2}\\) (where \\(k\\) is the radius of the inversion);\n* the circumcenter of a triangle \\(ABC\\) with \\(A\\) the centre of inversion is sent to the midpoint of the segment \\(B'C'\\).\n\nApply \\(\\mathcal I\\) to the whole configuration.\n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) become two distinct lines, denote them by \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \n  Both lines pass through the image \\(B'\\) of the point \\(B\\) (because \\(B\\) lies on both circles).\n\n* The points \\(C,D\\) go to \\(C',D'\\) with \\(C'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(D'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).\n\n* The circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is sent to the midpoint of \\(C'D'\\); denote this midpoint by \\(P'\\).\n\n* The line \\(AP\\) is invariant under the inversion (it passes through the centre), hence it still coincides with the line \\(AP'\\).\n\n* The second intersections \\(E,F\\) of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) become the intersections \\(E',F'\\) of the same line \\(AP'\\) with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) respectively.\n\n* The circle \\((BEF)\\) does **not** pass through \\(A\\); therefore it is transformed into another circle, denote it by \\((B'E'F')\\).\n\nAll directed angles are preserved by inversion (modulo \\(180^{\\circ}\\)). Consequently (6) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle(P'B',\\,C'B')=\\angle B'F'E'.}\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Geometry of the inverted picture  \n\nFrom (1) we have \\(MN\\perp AB\\).  \nSince inversion sends the line \\(MN\\) (which does **not** pass through \\(A\\)) to a circle passing through \\(A\\), the orthogonality \\(MN\\perp AB\\) translates into the fact that the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) are **perpendicular** at their common point \\(B'\\):\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}\\perp\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\quad\\text{at }B'.\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus the triangle \\(C'B'D'\\) is a right triangle with right angle at \\(B'\\).\n\nBecause \\(P'\\) is the midpoint of the hypotenuse \\(C'D'\\) of the right triangle \\(C'B'D'\\), the well\u2011known property of a right triangle yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(P'B',\\,C'B')=\\angle B'F'E'.\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, in a right triangle the segment joining the midpoint of the hypotenuse to the right\u2011angle vertex is equal to the radius of the circumcircle of the triangle; consequently the median \\(P'B'\\) is also a **circumradius** of \\(\\triangle C'B'D'\\).  \nSince \\(E'\\) and \\(F'\\) lie on the legs \\(C'B'\\) and \\(D'B'\\) respectively, the angle subtended by the chord \\(E'F'\\) at the point \\(B'\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the centre \\(P'\\), i.e. (9).  \n\nEquation (9) is precisely (7). Hence (7) holds, and by the chain of equivalences established in Sections\u202f3 and\u202f4 we obtain (6).  \n\nFinally, (6) is equivalent to the statement that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\). \u220e\n"
