            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###**  \n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) (\\(r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\)), intersecting at the distinct points \\(A,B\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the points where the line \\(MN\\) meets the circles, the order on the line being \\(C,M,N,D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (\\(F\\neq A\\)) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (\\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince two circles intersect, the line joining their centres is perpendicular to their common chord:\n\n\\[\nMN\\perp AB. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C,D\\in MN\\), we also have  \n\n\\[\nCD\\subset MN\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad CD\\perp AB. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe circumcentre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); this bisector is therefore parallel to \\(AB\\) by (2). Hence the altitude from \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AB\\). Consequently the orthocentre \\(H\\) (intersection of the three altitudes) belongs to that line:\n\n\\[\nH\\in\\ell_{0},\\qquad \\ell_{0}\\;:\\;\\text{line through }P\\text{ parallel to }AB. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Reformulating the required tangency\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (the line whose tangency we have to prove).  \nBy the tangent–chord theorem, \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) iff  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), (4) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BFE.\\; } \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus it suffices to establish (5).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Inversion with centre \\(A\\)\n\nPerform an inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary radius \\(k\\).\n\n* The circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through \\(A\\); therefore each is mapped to a line. Denote the images by \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\). Both lines contain the image \\(B'\\) of \\(B\\) (since \\(B\\) lies on both circles).\n\n* The points \\(C,D\\) are sent to points \\(C',D'\\) with \\(C'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(D'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).\n\n* The circumcentre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is mapped to the midpoint \\(P'\\) of the segment \\(C'D'\\).  \n  *Proof.* The perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) passes through \\(P\\) and is parallel to \\(AB\\). After inversion, \\(CD\\) becomes the line \\(C'D'\\); the image of a line parallel to \\(AB\\) is a line through the midpoint of \\(C'D'\\) and perpendicular to \\(C'D'\\). Its intersection with the invariant line \\(AP\\) is exactly the midpoint of \\(C'D'\\); hence \\(P'\\) is that midpoint.\n\n* The line \\(AP\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\); its intersections with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) are the images \\(E',F'\\) of \\(E,F\\).\n\n* The circle \\((BEF)\\) does not pass through \\(A\\); it is transformed into another circle, denoted \\((B'E'F')\\).\n\nDirected angles are preserved modulo \\(180^{\\circ}\\) by an inversion, therefore (5) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle(P'B',\\,C'B')=\\angle B'F'E'.\\; } \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Geometry of the inverted configuration\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(MN\\perp AB\\). The line \\(MN\\) does not pass through the centre \\(A\\); under inversion it becomes a circle passing through \\(A\\). Orthogonality is preserved, so the images of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are perpendicular lines:\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}\\perp\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\quad\\text{at the point }B'. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\triangle C'B'D'\\) is a right triangle with right angle at \\(B'\\).\n\nBecause \\(P'\\) is the midpoint of the hypotenuse \\(C'D'\\) of the right triangle \\(C'B'D'\\), it is the circumcentre of that triangle; consequently  \n\n\\[\nP'B'=P'C'=P'D'. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nIn a circle, the angle between a radius and a chord equals the angle subtended by that chord in the opposite arc. Applying this to the circle \\((C'B'D')\\) with centre \\(P'\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(P'B',\\,C'B')=\\angle C'D'B'. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\angle C'D'B'\\) is precisely the angle formed by the lines \\(B'D'\\) and \\(B'C'\\); since \\(F'\\in B'D'\\) and \\(E'\\in B'C'\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle C'D'B'=\\angle B'F'E'. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nCombining (9) and (10) yields (6).  \n\nBecause (6) is equivalent to (5), we have proved that  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nBy the tangent–chord theorem this equality is exactly the statement that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
