]  \n   * Under this inversion  \n     * the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (both passing through \\(A\\)) become two distinct lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) intersecting at the image \\(B'\\) of \\(B\\);  \n     * the points \\(C,D\\) go to points \\(C',D'\\) on \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) respectively;  \n     * the line \\(AP\\) is invariant, so its image is still the line \\(AP\\);  \n     * the points \\(E,F\\) go to the intersections \\(E',F'\\) of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\);  \n     * the circum‑centre \\(P\\) is sent to a point \\(P'\\) that **lies on the line \\(AP\\) and is the midpoint of the segment \\(C'D'\\)** (the special choice of \\(k\\) guarantees this, as shown below).  \n     * the circle \\((BEF)\\) is transformed into the circle \\((B'E'F')\\).\n\n   * Directed angles are preserved (mod \\(180^{\\circ}\\)) by an inversion, so (1) is equivalent to  \n     \\[\n     \\boxed{\\;\\angle(P'B',\\,C'B')=\\angle B'F'E'.\\;}\\tag{2}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Why the chosen radius makes \\(P'\\) the midpoint of \\(C'D'\\).**  \n   * Because \\(AP\\perp CD\\), the power of \\(A\\) with respect to the circle \\((ACD)\\) equals  \n     \\[\n     \\operatorname{Pow}_A(ACD)=AP\\cdot AB .\n     \\]  \n   * For an inversion of radius \\(k\\) centred at \\(A\\) the image of a point \\(X\\) satisfies \\(AX\\cdot AX'=k^{2}\\).  \n   * Taking \\(k^{2}=AP\\cdot AB\\) we obtain  \n     \\[\n     AC\\cdot AC'=k^{2}=AP\\cdot AB,\\qquad AD\\cdot AD'=k^{2}=AP\\cdot AB .\n     \\]  \n   * Hence \\(AC'=AB\\) and \\(AD'=AB\\); consequently the points \\(C',D'\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AB\\).  \n   * The line \\(AB\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(C'D'\\); therefore its intersection with the invariant line \\(AP\\) (which is perpendicular to \\(CD\\) and thus to \\(C'D'\\)) is exactly the midpoint of \\(C'D'\\).  \n   * This intersection is precisely the image \\(P'\\) of \\(P\\). Hence \\(