ine (see Lemma 1 below).\n\n3. **Re‑formulation of the tangency condition.**  \n   By the tangent–chord theorem, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) iff  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Directed angles are preserved (mod \\(180^{\\circ}\\)) by an inversion, hence (1) is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle (P'B',\\,C'B')=\\angle B'F'E'.\\;}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   In the inverted picture \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) play the rôle of the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\); thus (2) states that the line \\(AP\\) (still the line \\(AP\\) after inversion) bisects the angle formed by \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).\n\n4. **Key Lemma – image of a centre.**  \n\n   **Lemma 1.** *Let \\(\\mathcal I\\) be an inversion with centre \\(A\\) and radius \\(k\\).  \n   If a circle \\(\\omega\\) passes through \\(A\\) and has centre \\(O\\), then the image \\(O'\\) of \\(O\\) lies on the line \\(AO\\) and is the midpoint of the segment joining the two intersection points of \\(\\mathcal I(\\omega)\\) with the line \\(AO\\).*\n\n   *Proof.*  Because \\(\\omega\\) passes through the centre of the inversion, \\(\\mathcal I(\\omega)\\) is a line, say \\(\\ell\\).  For any point \\(X\\in\\omega\\) we have \\(AX\\cdot AX'=k^{2}\\).  In particular, for the two points \\(X_{1},X_{2}\\in\\omega\\) that lie on the line \\(AO\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   AX_{1}\\cdot AX_{1}'=AX_{2}\\cdot AX_{2}'=k^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(X_{1}'\\) and \\(X_{2}'\\) are symmetric with respect to the midpoint of the segment \\(X_{1}'X_{2}'\\), and that midpoint lies on the line \\(AO\\).  Since \\(O\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega\\), the points \\(X_{1},X_{2}\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(O\\); consequently their images \\(X_{1}',X_{2}'\\) are symmetric with respect to the image \\(O'\\).  Therefore \\(O'\\) is exactly the midpoint of \\(X_{1}'X_{2}'\\).  ∎\n\n   Applying Lemma 1 to 