\\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the points \\(E',F'\\) are the intersections of the invariant line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) respectively.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1.** *With the radius chosen as in (5) the image \\(P'\\) of the circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is the midpoint of the segment \\(C'D'\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe circle \\((ACD)\\) passes through the centre \\(A\\) of the inversion; therefore its image is a straight line, namely the line \\(C'D'\\).  \nLet \\(X\\) be any point of \\((ACD)\\) distinct from \\(A\\); then \\(AX\\cdot AX'=k^{2}\\).  \nApply this to the two points of \\((ACD)\\) that lie on the line \\(AO\\), where \\(O\\) denotes the centre of \\((ACD)\\) (i.e. \\(O=P\\)).  \nLet those points be \\(U\\) and \\(V\\); they are symmetric with respect to \\(P\\) because \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle.  \nConsequently \\(AU\\cdot AU'=AV\\cdot AV'=k^{2}\\) and the images \\(U',V'\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\nAU'=AV'=\\frac{k^{2}}{AU}= \\frac{k^{2}}{AV}.\n\\]\n\nHence \\(U'\\) and \\(V'\\) are symmetric with respect to the point \\(P'\\) that lies on the line \\(AP\\).  \nBut the line \\(C'D'\\) is precisely the line \\(U'V'\\) (the image of \\((ACD)\\)).  \nTherefore \\(P'\\) is the midpoint of the segment \\(U'V'=C'D'\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Geometry of the inverted configuration\n\nSummarising the images:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n& C'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad D'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma},\\\\\n& E'=AP\\cap\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad F'=AP\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma},\\\\\n& B'=\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma},\\qquad P' \\text{ is the midpoint of }C'D'.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C',D',B'\\) are three non‑collinear points, they determine a unique circle; denote it by \\(\\omega\\).  \nSince \\(P'\\) is the midpoint of the chord \\(C'D'\\) of \\(\\omega\\), it follows that \\(P'\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega\\); consequently\n\n\\[\nP'B'=P'C'=P'D'.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\omega\\) is the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle C'B'D'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Reformulation of the required tangency\n\nIn the original figure the statement to be proved is:\n\n> *The line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).*\n\nBy the tangent–chord theorem this is equivalent to\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE\\;}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nDirected angles are preserved (mod \\(180^{\\circ}\\)) by any inversion; therefore (8) holds in the original figure iff the corresponding equality holds after the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\).  \nUsing the correspondences (6) we obtain the equivalent condition\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (P'B',\\,C'B')=\\angle B'F'E'\\;}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Verification of (9)\n\nBecause of (7), the point \\(P'\\) is the centre of the circum‑circle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle C'B'D'\\).  \nHence the angle formed by the radius \\(P'B'\\) and the chord \\(B'C'\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at any point of the circle, in particular at \\(D'\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle (P'B',C'B')=\\angle C'D'B'.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBut the lines \\(B'D'\\) and \\(B'C'\\) are exactly the lines \\(B'F'\\) and \\(B'E'\\) (by the definition of \\(E',F'\\) in (6)).  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle C'D'B'=\\angle B'F'E'.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nCombining (10) and (11) yields (9).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion\n\nEquality (9) is equivalent to (8); therefore (8) holds in the original configuration.  \nBy the tangent–chord theorem, (8) states precisely that the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) which is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nHence the required tangency is proved."
