r,\\ |n|=R\\) (because \\(A=0\\) lies on\nboth circles) we obtain after a short simplification\n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{F}-\\lambda_{E}\n   =\\frac{(R^{2}-r^{2})\\,\n          \\bigl(\\Re(P\\overline{n})-\\Re(P\\overline{m})\\bigr)}\n          {|P|^{2}-2\\Re(P\\overline{m})+r^{2}}\n          \\;>\\;0 .\n\\]\n\nThe denominator is positive (it is \\(|P-m|^{2}\\)), and the numerator is\npositive because \\(R>r\\) and, by (1), \\(\\Re(P\\overline{n})>\\Re(P\\overline{m})\\).\nConsequently (10) holds, whence (9) and finally (7) are proved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Tangency\n\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\nEquation (7) says that the angle made by the line \\(AP\\) with the chord\n\\(BE\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the point \\(F\\) of\n\\(\\omega\\).  By the tangent–chord theorem, the line through the point\n\\(A\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at the point where it\nmeets \\(\\omega\\).\n\nThe orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on the line through \\(A\\)\nparallel to \\(AP\\) (see (6) and the definition of \\(H\\)); therefore the\nline through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly that tangent line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Conclusion  \n\nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is\nparallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required line is tangent to }(BEF).}\n\\]"
