7.  The line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe direction of \\(AP\\) is \\(\\vec v=(0.54,-1.27)\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;(x,y)=H+s\\vec v,\\qquad s\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\]\n\nIn Cartesian form, using the normal vector \\(\\vec n=(1.27,0.54)\\) (perpendicular to \\(\\vec v\\)),  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;1.27\\,x+0.54\\,y-0.3462=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Circumcircle \\((BEF)\\)\n\nThe three points are  \n\n\\[\nB=(-0.19,-0.982),\\quad\nE=(0.575,-0.817),\\quad\nF=(0.729,-1.180).\n\\]\n\nThe circum‑centre \\(O\\) is obtained as the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\).  \nCarrying out the elementary algebra (or using a computer‑algebra system) yields  \n\n\\[\nO=\\bigl(0.154,\\;-0.720\\bigr),\\qquad\nr=|O-B|=0.432\\;(\\text{approximately}).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.  Distance from \\(O\\) to the line \\(\\ell\\)\n\nThe distance from a point \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\) to the line \\(ax+by+c=0\\) equals  \n\n\\[\nd=\\frac{|ax_{0}+by_{0}+c|}{\\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}}.\n\\]\n\nHere \\(a=1.27,\\;b=0.54,\\;c=-0.3462\\) and \\((x_{0},y_{0})=O\\).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nd&=\\frac{|1.27\\cdot0.154+0.54\\cdot(-0.720)-0.3462|}\n          {\\sqrt{1.27^{2}+0.54^{2}}}\\\\[2mm]\n  &=\\frac{|0.1956-0.3888-0.3462|}{\\sqrt{1.6129+0.2916}}\\\\[2mm]\n  &=\\frac{0.5395}{\\sqrt{1.9045}}\\\\[2mm]\n  &=\\frac{0.5395}{1.38}=0.391\\;.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 10.  Comparison with the radius  \n\nThe radius of \\((BEF)\\) is \\(r\\approx0.432\\).  \nSince  \n\n\\[\nd\\;=\\;0.391\\;\\neq\\;0.432\\;=r,\n\\]\n\nthe line \\(\\ell\\) does **not** meet the circle \\((BEF)\\) in exactly one point; it meets it in two points (it cuts the circle). Consequently \\(\\ell\\) is **not** tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---------------------