btain the equalities of corresponding angles\n\n\\[\n\\angle CAD=\\angle EAF,\\qquad\n\\angle CDA=\\angle EFA .\n\\tag{1.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Geometry of the circle \\((ACD)\\)\n\nBy definition \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the central angle subtended by the chord \\(CD\\) is twice the inscribed\nangle that subtends the same chord:\n\n\\[\n\\angle CPD = 2\\angle CAD .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the midpoint of the segment \\(CD\\).  \nSince \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the line\nthrough \\(O\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  Because the centre of a circle lies on the\nperpendicular bisector of any of its chords, \\(P\\) belongs to this line; hence\n\n\\[\nPO\\perp MN .\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude issued from the vertex \\(P\\) is precisely the line\n\\(PO\\) (by (2.3)).  Therefore the orthocenter \\(H\\) lies on \\(PO\\):\n\n\\[\nH\\in PO .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The crucial angle equality\n\nWe now compute the angle between the line \\(AP\\) and the chord \\(BE\\).\n\nBecause \\(E\\) belongs to \\(\\Omega\\), the angle formed by \\(AP\\) and \\(BE\\) equals the\nangle formed by the chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AB\\) of the same circle \\(\\Omega\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BAE .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nUsing the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) (1.1)–(1.3) we have\n\\(\\angle BAE = \\angle CAD\\); indeed \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the ordered pair of chords\n\\((AB,AE)\\) to \\((AB,AD)\\), preserving the oriented angle.\n\nHence, by (1.5),\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle CAD .\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nNow apply (2.2):\n\n\\[\n\\angle CAD = 