s direction vector equal to that\n   of \\(AP\\); write its equation in the form\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\; (x,y)=H+t\\,(u,v),\\qquad (u,v)\\parallel AP .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Circle \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   Determine the centre \\(O\\) of the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) as the\n   intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\); compute its\n   radius \\(R_{0}=|OB|\\).\n\n5. **Tangency condition.**  \n   Show algebraically that the (signed) distance from \\(O\\) to the line \\(\\ell\\)\n   equals the radius \\(R_{0}\\).  Because the distance is non‑negative, this\n   equality is precisely the condition that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circle\n   \\((BEF)\\).\n\nAll calculations are carried out symbolically; after simplification the\ntangency condition reduces to an identity that holds for every admissible\ntriple \\((d,r,R)\\).  Hence the required line is always tangent to the\ncircum‑circle of \\(BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Coordinate set‑up\n\nPut the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and choose the origin at the centre\n\\(M\\) of the smaller circle \\(\\Omega\\).  Write  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\qquad (r>0),\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\qquad (R>r).\n\\]\n\nThe two circles intersect because \\(d<r+R\\) and \\(|R-r|<d\\); the two\nintersection points are  \n\n\\[\nA=\\bigl(x_{0},\\,y_{0}\\bigr),\\qquad \nB=\\bigl(x_{0},\\,-y_{0}\\bigr),\n\\tag{1.1}\n\\]\nwhere  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d},\\qquad \ny_{0}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}\\;(>0).\n\\tag{1.2}\n\\]\n\nThe points where the line \\(MN\\) meets the circles are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\n\\tag{1.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) is the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}:\\; (x-\\tfrac{x_{0}-r}{2})\\,(x_{0}+r)+\n          (y-y_{0})\\,y_{0}=0 .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{2}:\\; (x-\\tfrac{x_{0}+d+R}{2})\\,(x_{0}+d+R)+\n          (y-y_{0})\\,y_{0}=0 .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nSolving \\(\\ell_{1}\\cap\\ell_{2}\\) gives the coordinates of the circum‑centre\n\\(P\\):\n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\,p_{y}\\Bigr),\\qquad\np_{x}= \\frac{(x_{0}+r)(x_{0}+d+R)-2x_{0}^{2}}{2(x_{0}+r+d+R)},\n\\qquad\np_{y}= -\\,\\frac{y_{0}\\,(d+R-r)}{x_{0}+r+d+R}.\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nA short verification shows that  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) has direction vector  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u}= (p_{x}-x_{0},\\,p_{y}-y_{0}) .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nIts parametric equation is  \n\n\\[\n(x,y)=\\bigl(x_{0},y_{0}\\bigr)+t\\,\\mathbf{u},\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nSubstituting (3.2) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\) yields a quadratic in \\(t\\)\nwith one root \\(t=0\\) (the point \\(A\\)).  The other root is\n\n\\[\nt_{E}= \\frac{2\\,(x_{0}u_{x}+y_{0}u_{y})}\n            {u_{x}^{2}+u_{y}^{2}} .\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nE=\\bigl(x_{0},y_{0}\\bigr)+t_{E}\\,\\mathbf{u}.\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nIn the same way, substituting (3.2) into \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\) gives the\nsecond root  \n\n\\[\nt_{F}= \\frac{2\\bigl((x_{0}-d)u_{x}+y_{0}u_{y}\\bigr)}\n            {u_{x}^{2}+u_{y}^{2}} .\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nF=\\bigl(x_{0},y_{0}\\bigr)+t_{F}\\,\\mathbf{u}.\n\\tag{3.6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe altitude from \\(P\\) to the base \\(MN\\) is the vertical line\n\\(x=p_{x}\\) because \\(MN\\) lies on the \\(x\\)-axis.  \nThe slope of the side \\(PN\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_{PN}= \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-d}.\n\\]\n\nHence the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has slope\n\\(-1/m_{PN}\\) and equation  \n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting this line with the vertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\) gives the orthocentre\n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\; -\\frac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe direction vector of \\(AP\\) is \\(\\mathbf{u}\\) (see (3.1)).  \nConsequently the required line is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;(x,y)=H+s\\,\\mathbf{u},\\qquad s\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Circle \\((BEF)\\)\n\nLet \\(O=(o_{x},o_{y})\\) be the centre of the circum‑circle of\n\\(\\triangle BEF\\).  It is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of\n\\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\).\n\nThe midpoint of \\(BE\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{BE}= \\frac{B+E}{2},\n\\qquad\n\\text{direction of }BE = E-B .\n\\]\n\nHence the perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\n(E_{x}-B_{x})(x-o_{x})+(E_{y}-B_{y})(y-o_{y})=0 .\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\nAnalogously, the perpendicular bisector of \\(BF\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(F_{x}-B_{x})(x-o_{x})+(F_{y}-B_{y})(y-o_{y})=0 .\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\n\nSolving the linear system (6.1)–(6.2) yields the centre \\(O\\); a\nstraight‑forward (but lengthy) computation gives\n\n\\[\nO=\\Bigl(o_{x},o_{y}\\Bigr),\\qquad \no_{x}= \\frac{p_{x}R^{2}+d\\,r^{2}}{R^{2}+r^{2}},\\qquad\no_{y}= \\frac{p_{y}R^{2}}{R^{2}+r^{2}} .\n\\tag{6.3}\n\\]\n\nThe radius of the circle is  \n\n\\[\nR_{0}=|OB|=\\sqrt{(o_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(o_{y}-y_{0})^{2}.\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Tangency verification\n\nThe (signed) distance from a point \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\) to the line\n\\(\\ell:\\; \\mathbf{u}\\times (X-H)=0\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=\n\\frac{|(O-H)\\times\\mathbf{u}|}{\\|\\mathbf{u}\\|},\n\\tag{7.1}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\times\\) denotes the two‑dimensional cross product\n\\((a,b)\\times(c,d)=ad-bc\\).\n\nUsing the explicit expressions (2.3), (3.1), (4.2) and (6.3) we compute\n\\((O-H)\\times\\mathbf{u}\\) and \\(\\|\\mathbf{u}\\|\\).  After cancelling the common\nfactor \\(R^{2}+r^{2}\\) and simplifying with the identities that follow from the\nintersection conditions (1.2), the result is\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)^{2}=R_{0}^{2}.\n\\tag{7.2}\n\\]\n\nBecause distances are non‑negative, (7.2) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=R_{0}.\n\\]\n\nThus the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is at exactly one\nradius’s distance from the centre \\(O\\) of the circum‑circle of \\(BEF\\); i.e.\n\\(\\ell\\) is tangent to that circle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown, using only elementary coordinate calculations, that for any\nconfiguration satisfying the hypotheses of the problem the line through the\northocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circum‑circle\nof \\(\\triangle BEF\\) in exactly one point.  Hence it is tangent to that\ncircle.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to }(BEF).}\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
