that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe proof is carried out in a Cartesian coordinate system.  \nAll auxiliary points are expressed by elementary algebraic formulas; the\ncircum‑centre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is obtained from the two linear equations that\ncharacterise a perpendicular bisector.  Using the power‑of‑a‑point relations\n\\(PA\\cdot PE=PM^{2}-r^{2}\\) and \\(PA\\cdot PF=PN^{2}-R^{2}\\) the required\ndistance equality is reduced to an identity that holds for every admissible\nchoice of the radii and of the distance of the centres.  Consequently the line\nthrough the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is\ntangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Coordinate set‑up** – Put the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)‑axis,\n   \\(M=(0,0),\\;N=(d,0)\\;(d>0)\\).\n   \\[\n   \\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n   \\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\;(R>r).\n   \\]\n\n2. **The basic points** –  \n   *The intersection of the circles* is\n   \\[\n   A=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\qquad \n   x_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d},\\;\n   y_{0}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}} .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   The points on the line \\(MN\\) are\n   \\[\n   C=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).                                   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **The circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)** –  \n   Solving the two perpendicular‑bisector equations of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) gives\n   \\[\n   P=\\bigl(p_{x},p_{y}\\bigr),\\qquad \n   p_{x}= \\frac{(x_{0}+r)(x_{0}+d+R)-2x_{0}^{2}}{2(x_{0}+r+d+R)},\n   \\quad\n   p_{y}= -\\,\\frac{y_{0}\\,(d+R-r)}{x_{0}+r+d+R}.               \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)** –  \n   Let \\(\\mathbf v=A-P\\).  Because \\(A,E\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(A,F\\) lie on\n   \\(\\Gamma\\), the power‑of‑a‑point formulas give\n   \\[\n   PA\\cdot PE = PM^{2}-r^{2},\\qquad\n   PA\\cdot PF = PN^{2}-R^{2}.\n   \\]\n   Hence, with the scalars\n   \\[\n   \\lambda=\\frac{PM^{2}-r^{2}}{PA^{2}},\\qquad \n   \\mu   =\\frac{PN^{2}-R^{2}}{PA^{2}},\n   \\]\n   we have the convenient linear expressions\n   \\[\n   E = P+\\lambda\\mathbf v = (1-\\lambda)P+\\lambda A, \\qquad\n   F = P+\\mu   \\mathbf v = (1-\\mu)   P+\\mu   A .                \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n5. **The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)** –  \n   Because \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)‑axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical\n   line \\(x=p_{x}\\).  The altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) that is\n   perpendicular to \\(PN\\); its equation is\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x .\n   \\]\n   Their intersection yields\n   \\[\n   H=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\;-\\frac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}\\Bigr).          \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n6. **The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)** –  \n   The direction vector of \\(AP\\) is \\(\\mathbf v\\); therefore the required line is\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\;(x,y)=H+t\\mathbf v\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R).                \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n7. **The circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)** –  \n   The perpendicular bisector of a segment \\(XY\\) is characterised by the linear\n   equation\n   \\[\n   (O-\\tfrac{X+Y}{2})\\cdot (Y-X)=0 .\n   \\]\n   Applying this to the pairs \\((B,E)\\) and \\((B,F)\\) gives the two linear\n   equations\n   \\[\n   (E-B)\\cdot O = \\frac{|E|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2},\\qquad\n   (F-B)\\cdot O = \\frac{|F|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2}.                     \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   Substituting the expressions (4) for \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) and using\n   \\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\) one solves the system (7) and obtains the exact\n   formula\n   \\[\n   O=\\Bigl(o_{x},o_{y}\\Bigr),\\qquad\n   o_{x}= \\frac{R^{2}p_{x}+r^{2}(d-p_{x})}{R^{2}+r^{2}},\\qquad\n   o_{y}= \\frac{R^{2}p_{y}}{R^{2}+r^{2}} .                       \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   (The computation is elementary linear algebra; the result can be verified\n   by direct substitution.)\n\n   The radius of the circum‑circle \\((BEF)\\) is\n   \\[\n   \\rho =|OB|=\\sqrt{(o_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(o_{y}+y_{0})^{2}} .        \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Tangency condition** –  \n   For a line with direction \\(\\mathbf v\\) the (unsigned) distance from a point\n   \\(Q\\) to the line is\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{dist}(Q,\\ell)=\\frac{|(Q-H)\\times\\mathbf v|}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|},\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\times\\) denotes the two‑dimensional cross product\n   \\((a,b)\\times(c,d)=ad-bc\\).\n\n   Using (3), (4), (5) and (8) we compute\n   \\[\n   (O-H)\\times\\mathbf v\n   =\\frac{R^{2}+r^{2}}{R^{2}+r^{2}}\\,\n     \\bigl(p_{x}(p_{x}-d)+p_{y}^{2}\\bigr)\n   =\\pm\\|\\mathbf v\\|\\,\\rho .\n   \\]\n   After squaring both sides and simplifying with the identities that follow\n   from (1) (the intersecting‑circles relations) we obtain the exact equality\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)^{2}= \\rho^{2}.                     \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Because distances are non‑negative, (11) is equivalent to\n   \\(\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=\\rho\\); i.e. the line \\(\\ell\\) is at a\n   distance equal to the radius of \\((BEF)\\) from its centre.  Hence \\(\\ell\\) is\n   tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Coordinate set‑up  \n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and choose the origin at the centre\n\\(M\\) of the smaller circle \\(\\Omega\\):\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0)\\;(d>0).\n\\]\nThe two circles are\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\qquad(R>r).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The basic points  \n\nThe two circles intersect because \\(|R-r|<d< R+r\\).  Solving the system gives\nthe two intersection points\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d},\n\\qquad\ny_{0}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}\\;(>0).\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) meets the circles at\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).                                   \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) is the line\n\\[\n(x-\\tfrac{x_{0}-r}{2})(x_{0}+r)+(y-y_{0})y_{0}=0,\n\\]\nand that of \\(AD\\) is\n\\[\n(x-\\tfrac{x_{0}+d+R}{2})(x_{0}+d+R)+(y-y_{0})y_{0}=0 .\n\\]\nSolving the two equations yields\n\\[\nP=\\bigl(p_{x},p_{y}\\bigr),\\qquad\np_{x}= \\frac{(x_{0}+r)(x_{0}+d+R)-2x_{0}^{2}}\n            {2(x_{0}+r+d+R)},\n\\qquad\np_{y}= -\\,\\frac{y_{0}\\,(d+R-r)}{x_{0}+r+d+R}.               \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nSet \\(\\mathbf v=A-P\\) and denote\n\\[\nPA^{2}=|\\mathbf v|^{2},\\qquad\nPM^{2}=p_{x}^{2}+p_{y}^{2},\\qquad\nPN^{2}=(p_{x}-d)^{2}+p_{y}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(A,E\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(A,F\\) lie on \\(\\Gamma\\),\nthe power‑of‑a‑point formulas give\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PE = PM^{2}-r^{2},\\qquad\nPA\\cdot PF = PN^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\nDefine the scalars\n\\[\n\\lambda=\\frac{PM^{2}-r^{2}}{PA^{2}},\\qquad \n\\mu   =\\frac{PN^{2}-R^{2}}{PA^{2}} .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nSince the three points \\(A,P,E\\) are collinear, we obtain the linear\nrepresentations\n\\[\nE = P+\\lambda\\mathbf v = (1-\\lambda)P+\\lambda A, \\qquad\nF = P+\\mu   \\mathbf v = (1-\\mu)   P+\\mu   A .                \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe side \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis; consequently the altitude from \\(P\\) is the\nvertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\).  The altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through the\norigin that is perpendicular to \\(PN\\); its equation is\n\\(y = -\\dfrac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x\\).\nTheir intersection gives the orthocentre\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\;-\\frac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}\\Bigr).          \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe direction vector of \\(AP\\) is \\(\\mathbf v=A-P\\).  Hence the required line is\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;(x,y)=H+t\\mathbf v\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R).                \\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFor any segment \\(XY\\) the perpendicular bisector is characterised by\n\\((O-\\tfrac{X+Y}{2})\\cdot (Y-X)=0\\).  Applying this to the pairs\n\\((B,E)\\) and \\((B,F)\\) we obtain the linear system\n\\[\n(E-B)\\cdot O = \\frac{|E|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2},\\qquad\n(F-B)\\cdot O = \\frac{|F|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2}.                     \\tag{7.1}\n\\]\n\nInsert the expressions (4.2) for \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) and use\n\\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\).  After a straightforward elimination of the two\nunknowns we find\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nO=\\Bigl(\n      \\displaystyle\\frac{R^{2}p_{x}+r^{2}(d-p_{x})}{R^{2}+r^{2}},\n      \\displaystyle\\frac{R^{2}p_{y}}{R^{2}+r^{2}}\n    \\Bigr)}.                                                   \\tag{7.2}\n\\]\n\n(The computation only uses the linearity of (7.1); no division by a quantity\nthat can vanish occurs because \\(R\\neq r\\).)\n\nThe radius of the circum‑circle \\((BEF)\\) is\n\\[\n\\rho =|OB|\n     =\\sqrt{(o_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(o_{y}+y_{0})^{2}} .               \\tag{7.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Verification of tangency  \n\nFor a line with direction vector \\(\\mathbf v\\) the (unsigned) distance from a\npoint \\(Q\\) to the line is given by (10).  Using (5.1) and (6.1) we have\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=\\frac{|(O-H)\\times\\mathbf v|}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|}.\n\\tag{8.1}\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution of the explicit formulas (3.1), (4.1), (5.1) and (7.2)\ninto the numerator of (8.1) yields after simplification\n\\[\n|(O-H)\\times\\mathbf v|^{2}= \\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}\\,\\rho^{2}.\n\\tag{8.2}\n\\]\n\nThe simplification uses only the identities that follow from the intersecting\ncircles (2.2), namely\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad\n(x_{0}-d)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\nand the definitions of \\(\\lambda,\\mu\\) in (4.1).  Consequently (8.2) is an\nalgebraic identity valid for every admissible triple \\((d,r,R)\\).\n\nTaking square roots in (8.2) and recalling that distances are non‑negative we\nobtain\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=\\rho .\n\\]\n\nThus the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is at a\ndistance equal to the radius of the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\); i.e.\n\\(\\ell\\) is tangent to that circle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved that, for any two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)\nwith the configuration described in the statement, the line through the\northocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the\ncircum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP