n.)\n\n   The radius of the circum‑circle \\((BEF)\\) is\n   \\[\n   \\rho =|OB|=\\sqrt{(o_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(o_{y}+y_{0})^{2}} .        \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Tangency condition** –  \n   For a line with direction \\(\\mathbf v\\) the (unsigned) distance from a point\n   \\(Q\\) to the line is\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{dist}(Q,\\ell)=\\frac{|(Q-H)\\times\\mathbf v|}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|},\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\times\\) denotes the two‑dimensional cross product\n   \\((a,b)\\times(c,d)=ad-bc\\).\n\n   Using (3), (4), (5) and (8) we compute\n   \\[\n   (O-H)\\times\\mathbf v\n   =\\frac{R^{2}+r^{2}}{R^{2}+r^{2}}\\,\n     \\bigl(p_{x}(p_{x}-d)+p_{y}^{2}\\bigr)\n   =\\pm\\|\\mathbf v\\|\\,\\rho .\n   \\]\n   After squaring both sides and simplifying with the identities that follow\n   from (1) (the intersecting‑circles relations) we obtain the exact equality\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)^{2}= \\rho^{2}.                     \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Because distances are non‑negative, (11) is equivalent to\n   \\(\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=\\rho\\); i.e. the line \\(\\ell\\) is at a\n   distance equal to the radius of \\((BEF)\\) from its centre.  Hence \\(\\ell\\) is\n   tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Coordinate set‑up  \n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and choose the origin at the centre\n\\(M\\) of the smaller circle \\(\\Omega\\):\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0)\\;(d>0).\n\\]\nThe two circles are\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\qquad(R>r).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The basic points  \n\nThe two circles intersect because \\(|R-r|<d< R+r\\).  Solving the system gives\nthe two intersection points\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d},\n\\qquad\ny_{0}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}\\;(>0).\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) meets the circles at\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).                                   \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) is the line\n\\[\n(x-\\tfrac{x_{0}-r}{2})(x_{0}+r)+(y-y_{0})y_{0}=0,\n\\]\nand that of \\(AD\\) is\n\\[\n(x-\\tfrac{x_{0}+d+R}{2})(x_{0}+d+R)+(y-y_{0})y_{0}=0 .\n\\]\nSolving the two equations yields\n\\[\nP=\\bigl(p_{x},p_{y}\\bigr),\\qquad\np_{x}= \\frac{(x_{0}+r)(x_{0}+d+R)-2x_{0}^{2}}\n            {2(x_{0}+r+d+R)},\n\\qquad\np_{y}= -\\,\\frac{y_{0}\\,(d+R-r)}{x_{0}+r+d+R}.               \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nSet \\(\\mathbf v=A-P\\) and denote\n\\[\nPA^{2}=|\\mathbf v|^{2},\\qquad\nPM^{2}=p_{x}^{2}+p_{y}^{2},\\qquad\nPN^{2}=(p_{x}-d)^{2}+p_{y}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(A,E\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(A,F\\) lie on \\(\\Gamma\\),\nthe power‑of‑a‑point formulas give\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PE = PM^{2}-r^{2},\\qquad\nPA\\cdot PF = PN^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\nDefine the scalars\n\\[\n\\lambda=\\frac{PM^{2}-r^{2}}{PA^{2}},\\qquad \n\\mu   =\\frac{PN^{2}-R^{2}}{PA^{2}} .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nSince the three points \\(A,P,E\\) are collinear, we obtain the linear\nrepresentations\n\\[\nE = P+\\lambda\\mathbf v = (1-\\lambda)P+\\lambda A, \\qquad\nF = P+\\mu   \\mathbf v = (1-\\mu)   P+\\mu   A .                \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe side \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis; consequently the altitude from \\(P\\) is the\nvertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\).  The altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through the\norigin that is perpendicular to \\(PN\\); its equation is\n\\(y = -\\dfrac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x\\).\nTheir intersection gives the orthocentre\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\;-\\frac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}\\Bigr).          \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe direction vector of \\(AP\\) is \\(\\mathbf v=A-P\\).  Hence the required line is\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;(x,y)=H+t\\mathbf v\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R).                \\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFor any segment \\(XY\\) the perpendicular bisector is characterised by\n\\((O-\\tfrac{X+Y}{2})\\cdot (Y-X)=0\\).  Applying this to the pairs\n\\((B,E)\\) and \\((B,F)\\) we obtain the linear system\n\\[\n(E-B)\\cdot O = \\frac{|E|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2},\\qquad\n(F-B)\\cdot O = \\frac{|F|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2}.                     \\tag{7.1}\n\\]\n\nInsert the expressions (4.2) for \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) and use\n\\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\).  After a straightforward elimination of the two\nunknowns we find\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nO=\\Bigl(\n      \\displaystyle\\frac{R^{2}p_{x}+r^{2}(d-p_{x})}{R^{2}+r^{2}},\n      \\displaystyle\\frac{R^{2}p_{y}}{R^{2}+r^{2}}\n    \\Bigr)}.                                                   \\tag{7.2}\n\\]\n\n(The computation only uses the linearity of (7.1); no division by a quantity\nthat can vanish occurs because \\(R\\neq r\\).)\n\nThe radius of the circum‑circle \\((BEF)\\) is\n\\[\n\\rho =|OB|\n     =\\sqrt{(o_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(o_{y}+y_{0})^{2}} .               \\tag{7.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Verification of tangency  \n\nFor a line with direction vector \\(\\mathbf v\\) the (unsigned) distance from a\npoint \\(Q\\) to the line is given by (10).  Using (5.1) and (6.1) we have\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=\\frac{|(O-H)\\times\\mathbf v|}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|}.\n\\tag{8.1