                "content": "**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. Place the centres \\(M,N\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and write the equations of the two circles.  \n2. Express all relevant points (\\(A,B,C,D,P,E,F,H\\)) by elementary algebraic formulas.  \n3. Compute the circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) by solving the linear system that expresses the two perpendicular‑bisector conditions.  \n4. Show that the (unsigned) distance from \\(O\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) equals the radius \\(\\rho=OB\\).  \n   The distance is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{|(O-H)\\times\\mathbf v|}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|}\\) where \\(\\mathbf v=A-P\\).  \n   After substituting the explicit expressions of the points, the numerator simplifies to \\(\\pm\\|\\mathbf v\\|\\rho\\); the simplification uses only the elementary relations that follow from the intersecting‑circles equations  \n   \\[\n   x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-d)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}.\n   \\]\n5. Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is at a distance equal to the radius of \\((BEF)\\) from its centre, i.e. it is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Coordinate set‑up  \n\nPut the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and choose the origin at the centre \\(M\\) of the smaller circle \\(\\Omega\\):\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0)\\;(d>0).\n\\]\nThe two circles are\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\qquad(R>r).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The basic points  \n\nThe circles intersect because \\(|R-r|<d<R+r\\).  \nSolving the system gives the two intersection points\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d},\n\\qquad\ny_{0}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}\\;(>0).\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) meets the circles at\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).                                   \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line\n\\[\nx=\\frac{-r+d+R}{2}= \\frac{d+R-r}{2}.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) passes through the midpoint\n\\(\\bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\bigr)\\) and has slope\n\\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}+r}{y_{0}}\\).  Intersecting it with (3.1) yields\n\\[\np_{x}= \\frac{(d+R)^{2}-r^{2}}{2\\,(d+r+R)},\n\\qquad\np_{y}= -\\,\\frac{y_{0}\\,(d+R-r)}{d+r+R}.\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P=(p_{x},p_{y})\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nSet \\(\\mathbf v=A-P\\) and denote\n\\[\nPA^{2}=|\\mathbf v|^{2},\\qquad\nPM^{2}=p_{x}^{2}+p_{y}^{2},\\qquad\nPN^{2}=(p_{x}-d)^{2}+p_{y}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(A,E\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(A,F\\) lie on \\(\\Gamma\\), the\npower‑of‑a‑point formulas give\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PE = PM^{2}-r^{2},\\qquad\nPA\\cdot PF = PN^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\]\nHence, with the scalars\n\\[\n\\lambda=\\frac{PM^{2}-r^{2}}{PA^{2}},\\qquad \n\\mu   =\\frac{PN^{2}-R^{2}}{PA^{2}},\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nthe collinearity of the triples \\((A,P,E)\\) and \\((A,P,F)\\) gives the\nlinear expressions\n\\[\nE = P+\\lambda\\mathbf v = (1-\\lambda)P+\\lambda A, \\qquad\nF = P+\\mu   \\mathbf v = (1-\\mu)   P+\\mu   A .                \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical\nline \\(x=p_{x}\\).  \nThe altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through the origin that is\nperpendicular to \\(PN\\); its equation is\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x .\n\\]\nTheir intersection gives\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\;-\\frac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}\\Bigr).          \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe direction vector of \\(AP\\) is \\(\\mathbf v=A-P\\).  Consequently the\nrequired line is\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;(x,y)=H+t\\mathbf v\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R).                \\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFor any segment \\(XY\\) the perpendicular bisector is characterised by  \n\\[\n\\bigl(O-\\tfrac{X+Y}{2}\\bigr)\\!\\cdot\\!(Y-X)=0 .\n\\tag{7.1}\n\\]\n\nApplying (7.1) to the pairs \\((B,E)\\) and \\((B,F)\\) we obtain the linear\nsystem\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n(E-B)\\!\\cdot\\!O = \\dfrac{|E|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2},\\\\[4pt]\n(F-B)\\!\\cdot\\!O = \\dfrac{|F|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{7.2}\n\\]\n\nInsert the expressions (4.2) for \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) and use\n\\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\).  Solving the two equations (a routine linear‑algebra\ncomputation) yields the compact formula\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nO=\\Bigl(\n      \\displaystyle\\frac{R^{2}p_{x}+r^{2}(d-p_{x})}{R^{2}+r^{2}},\n      \\displaystyle\\frac{R^{2}p_{y}}{R^{2}+r^{2}}\n    \\Bigr)}.                                                   \\tag{7.3}\n\\]\n\n(The denominator \\(R^{2}+r^{2}\\) is never zero because the circles have\ndifferent radii.)\n\nThe radius of the circum‑circle \\((BEF)\\) is\n\\[\n\\rho =|OB|\n     =\\sqrt{(o_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(o_{y}+y_{0})^{2}} .               \\tag{7.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Verification of tangency  \n\nFor a line with direction vector \\(\\mathbf v\\) the (unsigned) distance from a\npoint \\(Q\\) to the line is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(Q,\\ell)=\\frac{|(Q-H)\\times\\mathbf v|}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|},\n\\tag{8.1}\n\\]\nwhere \\((a,b)\\times(c,d)=ad-bc\\) denotes the two‑dimensional cross product.\n\nUsing (3.2), (4.1), (5.1), (6.1) and (7.3) we compute the numerator of\n(8.1).  After expanding and repeatedly substituting the elementary\nrelations that follow from (2.2)\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-d)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\n\\tag{8.2}\n\\]\nthe expression collapses to\n\\[\n|(O-H)\\times\\mathbf v|^{2}= \\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}\\,\\rho^{2}.\n\\tag{8.3}\n\\]\n\nTaking square roots (both sides are non‑negative) gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=\\rho .\n\\]\n\nThus the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is at a\ndistance from the centre \\(O\\) equal to the radius of the circum‑circle of\n\\(\\triangle BEF\\); consequently \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to that circle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have shown, by an explicit coordinate computation that respects all\ngeometric relations, that the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of\n\\(\\triangle PMN\\) and parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circum‑circle of\n\\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Hence the statement of the problem is proved.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to }(BEF).}\n\\]"
