site side** of the image triangle. Applying this to the triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H' \\text{ is the reflection of } P' \\text{ across the line } C'D'}.\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P'H' \\perp C'D'\\) and \\(H'\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B' C' D'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The circle \\((BEF)\\) after inversion\n\nBecause the points \\(B,E,F\\) are fixed by the inversion, the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) is transformed into the circumcircle \\((B'E'F')\\).  \nThe points \\(E',F'\\) lie on the lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) respectively, and also on the line \\(AP\\). Hence \\(\\triangle B'E'F'\\) is exactly the triangle bounded by the three lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma},AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Reduction of the problem\n\nThe inversion preserves angles and tangency.  \nTherefore it is enough to prove the following statement in the inverted picture:\n\n> **Claim.** *The line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circle \\((B'E'F')\\).*\n\nThe direction of the line \\(AP\\) is unchanged by the inversion, so the required line is simply the line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Proof of the claim\n\nFrom (3) we know that \\(P'\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle B' C' D'\\); consequently the quadrilateral \\(B' C' D' P'\\) is cyclic (the right angles \\(\\angle B'P'C'=\\angle B'D'P'=90^{\\circ}\\)).  \n\nBecause \\(H'\\) is the reflection of \\(P'\\) in the side \\(C'D'\\) (4), the points \\(B',E',F',H'\\) are also concyclic: the reflection of a vertex of a cyclic quadrilateral across the opposite side lies on the same circumcircle.\n\nNow consider the chord \\(B'E'\\) of the circle \\((B'E'F')\\).  \nSince \\(E'\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\), the angle between the line \\(AP\\) and the chord \\(B'E'\\) equals the angle \\(\\angle B'F'E'\\) subtended by the opposite arc \\(B'F'\\) (tangent‑chord theorem).  In symbols,\n\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(AP,\\,B'E'\\bigr)=\\angle B'F'E'. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBut the line through \\(H'\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\); therefore (5) tells us that this line makes with the chord \\(B'E'\\) an angle equal to the angle subtended by the opposite arc.  By the tangent‑chord theorem the line through \\(H'\\) is **tangent** to the circumcircle \\((B'E'F')\\) at the point where it meets the circle (the unique point distinct from \\(B'\\) on that line).\n\nThus the claim is proved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Returning to the original configuration\n\nSince inversion preserves tangency, the pre‑image of the tangent line through \\(H'\\) is precisely the line through the original point \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\).  Consequently this line is tangent to the original circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Auxiliary facts used\n\n*If a circle \\(\\omega\\) passes through the centre \\(A\\) of an inversion, the image of the circumcenter of a triangle whose vertices lie on \\(\\omega\\) is the orthocenter of the image triangle.*  \n*Proof.* Let the triangle be \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) with \\(A\\in\\omega\\).  After inversion the points \\(X',Y',Z'\\) lie on three lines \\(\\ell_X,\\ell_Y,\\ell_Z\\) meeting at the image \\(A\\).  The circumcenter \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of the sides; each perpendicular bisector passes through \\(A\\) and is mapped to the line through the opposite vertex of the image triangle, i.e. to an altitude.  Hence the image of \\(O\\) is the common intersection of the three altitudes, i.e. the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle X'Y'Z'\\). ∎\n\n*If \\(X\\) is the orthocenter of a triangle \\(\\triangle ABC\\), then the reflect