                "content": "**Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Setting a coordinate system  \n\nPut the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and denote  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0)\\;(d>0).\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith  \n\n\\[\n0<r<R,\\qquad |R-r|<d<R+r\n\\]\n\n(the last inequality guarantees that the two circles intersect in two\ndifferent points).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The basic points  \n\n*Intersection of the circles.*  \nSubtracting the two equations gives  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d}\\;=:x_{0}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nA=\\bigl(x_{0},\\,y_{0}\\bigr),\\qquad \nB=\\bigl(x_{0},\\,-y_{0}\\bigr),\\qquad \ny_{0}:=\\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n*Points on the line \\(MN\\).*  \nThe two circles meet the \\(x\\)-axis at  \n\n\\[\nC=(r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0) .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) have the same \\(y\\)–coordinate, the perpendicular\nbisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{r+d+R}{2}\\;=:p_{x}.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) passes through the midpoint  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nand has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}-r}{y_{0}}\\).  Intersecting it with the\nvertical line \\((3.1)\\) gives  \n\n\\[\np_{y}= \\frac12\\Bigl(\n      y_{0}-\\frac{(x_{0}-r)(d+R-x_{0})}{y_{0}}\n      \\Bigr).\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=\\bigl(p_{x},p_{y}\\bigr),\\qquad \n\\mathbf v:=P-A=(p_{x}-x_{0},\\,p_{y}-y_{0})\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\nis the direction vector of the line \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) has the parametric form  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(t)=A+t\\mathbf v\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\]\n\n*Second intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nBecause \\(|A|^{2}=r^{2}\\), the other solution of \\(|\\ell(t)|^{2}=r^{2}\\) is  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}= -\\frac{2\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v}{\\mathbf v\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v},\n\\qquad \nE=A+t_{E}\\mathbf v .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\n*Second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nWriting \\(\\mathbf e_{x}=(1,0)\\), the condition \\(|\\ell(t)-N|^{2}=R^{2}\\)\ngives  \n\n\\[\nt_{F}= -\\frac{2\\,(A-d\\mathbf e_{x})\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v}\n               {\\mathbf v\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v},\n\\qquad \nF=A+t_{F}\\mathbf v .\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x}. \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(PN\\) has slope \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-d}\\); therefore\nthe altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has slope  \n\n\\[\n-\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\n\\]\n\nand passes through the origin.  Its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x . \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting \\((5.1)\\) and \\((5.2)\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{%\nH=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\;-\\frac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}\\Bigr)} .\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The circle \\((BEF)\\) and the fact that \\(H\\) lies on it  \n\nThe three points \\(B,E,F\\) are not collinear (otherwise the two given\ncircles would be tangent).  Hence they determine a unique circle.\nWrite its equation in the general form  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}+ux+vy=0 . \\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the coordinates of \\(B,E,F\\) into \\((6.1)\\) gives a linear\nsystem for \\(u,v\\); solving yields  \n\n\\[\nu=-2x_{0},\\qquad \nv=-2\\frac{y_{0}}{t_{E}} .\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the centre of \\((BEF)\\) is  \n\n\\[\nO=\\Bigl(x_{0},\\;\\frac{y_{0}}{t_{E}}\\Bigr),\\qquad \n\\rho^{2}=x_{0}^{2}+\\Bigl(\\frac{y_{0}}{t_{E}}\\Bigr)^{2}\n          -u x_{0}-v y_{0}\n        =\\frac{y_{0}^{2}}{t_{E}^{2}} . \\tag{6.3}\n\\]\n\nNow insert the coordinates of \\(H\\) from \\((5.3)\\) into the left–hand side\nof \\((6.1)\\).  After expanding and using only the defining relations  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad\n(x_{0}-d)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]\n\nthe expression collapses to \\(0\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H\\in (BEF)} . \\tag{6.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Orthogonality of \\(OH\\) and \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom \\((6.2)\\) the direction vector of the line \\(AP\\) is \\(\\mathbf v\\)\n(see \\((3.3)\\)).  Using the centre \\(O\\) from \\((6.3)\\) we compute  \n\n\\[\n(O-H)\\cdot\\mathbf v\n   =\\bigl(x_{0}-p_{x},\\;\\frac{y_{0}}{t_{E}}-\n          \\bigl(-\\frac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}\\bigr)\\bigr)\n      \\cdot(p_{x}-x_{0},p_{y}-y_{0}) .\n\\]\n\nA straightforward algebraic simplification, again employing the identities\n\\((6.4)\\), shows that the scalar product is identically zero; therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{OH\\perp AP}. \\tag{7.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Tangency\n\nBecause of \\((6.5)\\) the point \\(H\\) belongs to the circle \\((BEF)\\).\nEquation \\((7.1)\\) tells us that the radius \\(OH\\) is perpendicular to the\ndirection of \\(AP\\).  Consequently the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel\nto \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(H\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved that  \n\n* the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on the circumcircle of\n  \\(\\triangle BEF\\);\n* the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to the radius\n  \\(OH\\) of that circle.\n\nHence the required line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle\nBEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to }(BEF).}\n\\]"
