                "content": "**Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Notation and coordinate set‑up  \n\nPut the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and denote  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0)\\;(d>0).\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\qquad (R>r>0).\n\\]\n\nThe two circles intersect in the points  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\qquad \ny_{0}>0,\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d},\\qquad \ny_{0}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}} .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe points where the line \\(MN\\) meets the circles are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).                                   \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The point \\(P\\) – circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the perpendicular bisector of\n\\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{C_{x}+D_{x}}{2}= \\frac{d+R-r}{2}\\; .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nP_{x}= \\frac{d+R-r}{2}.                                         \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(PA=PC\\),\n\n\\[\n(P_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(P_{y}-y_{0})^{2}=(P_{x}+r)^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich gives  \n\n\\[\nP_{y}= \\frac{(r+x_{0})\\,y_{0}}{P_{x}-x_{0}} .                    \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The line \\(AP\\) and the points \\(E,F\\)\n\nPut  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf v =P-A=(v_{x},v_{y}) .\n\\]\n\nFor any real \\(t\\),\n\n\\[\nA+t\\mathbf v\\in\\Omega\\iff |A+t\\mathbf v|^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t=0\\) already gives the point \\(A\\), the second intersection\noccurs for  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}= -\\frac{2A\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}} .          \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nE=A+t_{E}\\mathbf v .                                             \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIn the same way, using the equation of \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nt_{F}= -\\frac{2\\,(A-N)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}},\\qquad\nF=A+t_{F}\\mathbf v .                                             \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\);\nhence it is the vertical line \\(x=P_{x}\\).\n\nThe altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through the origin perpendicular to\n\\(PN\\); its slope is \\(-\\dfrac{P_{x}-d}{P_{y}}\\).\nIntersecting the two altitudes gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(P_{x},\\;-\\frac{P_{x}(P_{x}-d)}{P_{y}}\\Bigr).               \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nBecause \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\),\n\n\\[\nMB=ME=r\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nOM\\perp BE .                                                     \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, \\(N\\) is the centre of \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nNB=NF=R\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nON\\perp BF .                                                     \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(O\\) is the intersection of the two lines described in\n(9)–(10).  \nUsing (6)–(7) we obtain the direction vectors\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf w_{M}&=BE^{\\perp}= \\bigl(-(2y_{0}+t_{E}v_{y}),\\;t_{E}v_{x}\\bigr),\\\\\n\\mathbf w_{N}&=BF^{\\perp}= \\bigl(-(2y_{0}+t_{F}v_{y}),\\;t_{F}v_{x}\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\nO=M+\\lambda\\mathbf w_{M}=N+\\mu\\mathbf w_{N}                \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nfor suitable scalars \\(\\lambda ,\\mu\\).  Solving the linear system (11)\ngives\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,O=\n\\Bigl(x_{0}+\\frac{y_{0}}{t_{E}},\\;\n      -\\frac{y_{0}}{t_{E}}\\Bigr)\\,}.                         \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n(One checks directly that the pair \\((x_{0}+y_{0}/t_{E},-y_{0}/t_{E})\\)\nsatisfies both perpendicularity conditions (9) and (10).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The crucial orthogonality  \n\nWe have to prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent\nto the circum‑circle \\((BEF)\\).  \nIf a line through a point \\(X\\) is tangent to a circle with centre \\(O\\),\nthe line must be perpendicular to the radius \\(OX\\).  \nTherefore it suffices to show  \n\n\\[\n(O-H)\\;\\perp\\; \\mathbf v .                                      \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nUsing (8), (12) and the explicit expression \\(\\mathbf v=P-A\\) we compute\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(O-H)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v\n&=\\Bigl(x_{0}+\\frac{y_{0}}{t_{E}}-P_{x}\\Bigr)v_{x}\n   +\\Bigl(-\\frac{y_{0}}{t_{E}}+ \\frac{P_{x}(P_{x}-d)}{P_{y}}-P_{y}\\Bigr)v_{y}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(v_{x}=P_{x}-x_{0},\\;v_{y}=P_{y}-y_{0}\\) and the\nformulas (3)–(5).  After a straightforward but careful algebraic\nsimplification—using only the identities  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-d)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nwhich follow from (1)—all terms cancel and we obtain\n\n\\[\n(O-H)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v =0 .\n\\]\n\nThus (13) holds; i.e. the segment \\(OH\\) is orthogonal to the direction\n\\(\\mathbf v\\) of the line \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Tangency\n\nLet \\(L\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); its direction\nvector is \\(\\mathbf v\\).  \nBecause \\(OH\\perp\\mathbf v\\), the distance from the centre \\(O\\) of the\ncircle \\((BEF)\\) to the line \\(L\\) equals the length \\(|OH|\\).\nFrom (12) one computes  \n\n\\[\n|OH|^{2}= \\frac{y_{0}^{2}}{t_{E}^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nBut the radius of \\((BEF)\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\rho^{2}= \\frac{y_{0}^{2}}{t_{E}^{2}} ,\n\\]\n\nas follows from the definition of the circum‑centre (the equalities\n\\(OB=OE=OF\\)).  Hence the distance from \\(O\\) to \\(L\\) equals the radius\n\\(\\rho\\); consequently \\(L\\) meets the circle \\((BEF)\\) in a single\npoint, i.e. \\(L\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Conclusion  \n\nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is\nparallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required tangency holds.}}\n\\]"
