\\(D\\in\\Gamma\\cap MN\\) is the point of \\(\\Gamma\\) on the side of \\(N\\).\n  Thus \\(C,M,N,D\\) are collinear in this order.\n\n* \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); consequently  \n\n  \\[\n  PA=PC=PD,\\qquad P\\in\\operatorname{perp\\,bis}(CD).\n  \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) is the second intersection of the line\n  \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).\n\n* \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Inversion at \\(A\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal I\\) be the inversion with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary\nradius \\(k>0\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\neq A\\) we denote its image by \\(X'=\\mathcal I(X)\\);\nby definition \\(X'\\) lies on the ray \\(AX\\) and  \n\n\\[\nAX\\cdot AX'=k^{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nImportant consequences of the inversion:\n\n* Because \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through \\(A\\), their images are\n  straight lines; we denote them by  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell:=\\mathcal I(\\Omega),\\qquad m:=\\mathcal I(\\Gamma).\n  \\tag{6}\n  \\]\n\n* The line \\(AP\\) is invariant under the inversion (it passes through the\n  centre); the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are sent to the points at infinity\n  in the direction of \\(AP\\).  Hence  \n\n  \\[\n  E',F' \\text{ are the points at infinity on the direction }AP.\n  \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n\n* The circum‑circle \\((BEF)\\) contains the two points at infinity\n  \\(E',F'\\); therefore its image is the straight line through\n  \\(B'\\) parallel to \\(AP\\):\n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal I\\bigl((BEF)\\bigr)=\\ell_{B}:=\\{\\,X\\mid X\\in\\text{ line through }B'\n  \\text{ parallel to }AP\\,\\}.\n  \\tag{8}\n  \\]\n\nThus the original tangency statement is equivalent to\n\n\\[\n\\text{the line through }H'\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ coincides with }\n\\ell_{B}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince both lines are parallel to \\(AP\\), (9) holds precisely when\n\\(H'\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{B}\\); i.e. when \\(B',H'\\) are joined by a line\nparallel to \\(AP\\).  Because \\(B'\\in AB\\) and \\(H'\\in AH\\), condition (9)\nis equivalent to\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;AP\\text{ is the external angle bisector of }\\angle BAH\\;}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly (2).  Hence it suffices to prove (2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Proof that \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle BAH\\)\n\nWe work in the original (non‑inverted) figure.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the reflection of the orthocentre \\(H\\) in the side\n\\(MN\\) lies on the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  This is a standard property of the orthocentre: the reflection\nof \\(H\\) across any side of a triangle lies on the circum‑circle of that\ntriangle. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nLet \\(H_{1}\\) be the reflection of \\(H\\) in the line \\(MN\\).  \nThen \\(AH_{1}=AP\\).\n\n*Proof.*  By Lemma 1, \\(H_{1}\\) belongs to the circum‑circle of\n\\(\\triangle PMN\\), whose centre is \\(P\\).  Hence \\(PH_{1}=PA\\).\nBecause \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(HH_{1}\\),\nthe quadrilateral \\(AH_{1}PH\\) is a kite with \\(PH=PH_{1}=PA\\); therefore\n\\(AH_{1}=AP\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3  \nPoints \\(A,B,H_{1}\\) are collinear.\n\n*Proof.*  The line \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the chord\n\\(AB\\) (see (3)), so the reflection of \\(B\\) in \\(MN\\) is the point\n\\(B_{1}\\) such that \\(AB_{1}=AB\\) and \\(AB_{1}\\) lies on the same line as\n\\(AB\\).  Because the reflection in a line sends a point to the unique\npoint symmetric with respect to that line, the reflection of \\(B\\) in\n\\(MN\\) is precisely the point \\(H_{1}\\) defined in Lemma 1:\nindeed, \\(B\\) and \\(H\\) are symmetric with respect to the line through\n\\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\) (the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\));\nreflecting once more in \\(MN\\) sends \\(B\\) to \\(H_{1}\\).  Consequently\n\\(A,B,H_{1}\\) are collinear. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4  \n\\( \\displaystyle \\angle BAP = \\angle PAH .\\)\n\n*Proof.*  By Lemma 2 we have \\(AP=AH_{1}\\); by Lemma 3 the points\n\\(B,A,H_{1}\\) are collinear, hence the ray \\(AB\\) coincides with the ray\n\\(AH_{1}\\).  Therefore the two oriented angles \\(\\angle BAP\\) and\n\\(\\angle PAH_{1}\\) are equal.  Since \\(H_{1}\\) is the reflection of \\(H\\)\nin the line \\(MN\\) and \\(MN\\perp AB\\) (by (3)), the reflection does not\nchange the magnitude of the angle with the line \\(AP\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\angle PAH_{1}= \\angle PAH .\n\\]\n\nCombining the equalities we obtain \\(\\angle BAP=\\angle PAH\\), which is\nexactly (2). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nWith Lemma 4 we have proved (2), hence (10) holds.  By the discussion of\nSection 3 this implies (9), i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to\n\\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nThe inversion centred at the common point \\(A\\) transforms the problem\ninto the elementary statement that \\(AP\\) is the external angle bisector\nof \\(\\angle BAH\\).  Lemmas 1–4, which rely only on well‑known\nproperties of orthocentres and on the fact that \\(MN\\) is the\nperpendicular bisector of the common chord \\(AB\\), establish this angle‑\nbisector property.  Consequently the required tangency follows.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to }(BEF).}\n\\]"
